WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: Begin with

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.140
character, end with character, find the story
in-between.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.140
Jill Chamberlain, Screenwriter and Author
of THE NUTSHELL TECHNIQUE-Crack the Secret

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.990
of Successful Screenwriting: Start with that
great premise “What if this happened?”

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:12.320
And then ask yourself “Okay, who would be
the most interesting character to put into

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.660
that dilemma?”

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.430
Film Courage: Do writers start with character
or plot?

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.670
Jill Chamberlain: You know I sometimes say
there are two kinds of writers, the ones who

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.160
start with character and the ones that start
with plot.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.440
I think both are valid ways.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.790
I think however that if someone starts with
character I wouldn’t tell them that they’re

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.700
wrong and that they shouldn’t.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:40.620
I think that if you start with plot it’s
a little easier to break story and to work

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.660
with story.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:49.950
So the example I was saying with GROUNDHOG
DAY, if you start with the premise is far

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.760
more interesting than the character.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.710
What if somebody woke up and it’s the same
day a second time?

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.500
That’s a great premise, right?

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.300
I want to start with that and then ask myself
well who would be the most interesting character

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.740
to put in that?

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.930
What if it was somebody that was naive?

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.030
That probably wouldn’t work.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.800
What if it’s somebody who is a jerk?

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.920
Ahh…now that helps me build the plot and
character right?

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:24.220
So for me it works so much more naturally
and it’s much more easy to work with story

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.940
to start that way.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.050
That said I know a lot of people who start
with character.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.422
They love to explore character and for a variety
of reasons.

00:01:32.422 --> 00:01:37.470
Sometimes they came from an acting background
and that is part of acting training sometimes.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.310
They like to get inside of a character’s
head.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.910
Maybe they are doing a biographical figure
and kind of have to in a way.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:50.380
You are starting with the character but then
you need to decide what plot moments do I

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:56.659
want to use to help show the character’s
change and what plot moments am I going to

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:01.810
leave out, then you kind of are necessarily
starting with character.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.990
But generally speaking if I had to pick one
of the two I would say that plot is the most

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:14.100
easy to manipulate and to build story around
and to make sure you are creating a satisfying

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.310
story.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:21.069
Start with that great premise, what if this
happened and then ask yourself “Okay, now

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:24.170
who would be the most interesting character
to put into that dilemma?”

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:29.500
Scott Myers: Begin with character, end with
character, find the story in between.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:35.780
I don’t care what paradigm people have with
story structure, how you get there is critical.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.830
So you start with your characters, that’s
my take.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.069
Again there is not one right way to do this.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:49.400
I’m just saying that this is a smart way
to go about it because the characters, it’s

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.400
their story.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:56.010
If we’re writing a story, we are assuming
that in some magical, mystical way that story

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.560
some how exists, those characters exist.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.709
And if it’s their story, they know it better
than we do and so character development is

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:08.599
about getting curious, it’s about asking
questions, it’s about spending time with

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:15.681
the characters and so the typical things that
we see like biographies, questionnaires, those

00:03:15.681 --> 00:03:16.681
are great.

00:03:16.681 --> 00:03:20.160
I call those Indirect Engagement Exercises
because you are reflecting on the character,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.599
you are thinking about the character.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:25.599
As opposed to Direct Engagement Exercises.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:33.879
For example, what if I have a character in
mind, I am going to imagine that I am a psychiatrist,

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:39.140
this character is my patient and if they’re
reticent to talk to me I can even go one step

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.140
further.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.900
They have been court appointed to appear with
me as a psychiatrist and they have to answer

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.480
my questions and if they don’t they will
stay stuck in the situation until they do.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.410
So you just create a situation in which what
I was saying earlier just ask questions, get

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:58.709
curious, that was a lesson I learned when
I was an Air Force brat you just ask people

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:03.510
questions and get them talking and so you
type, you just let it go.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:10.090
You can even do something that is a little
bit spiritual in a way where you are going

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.720
to do either a monologue or stream of consciousness
with the character.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.209
It’s sort of like the Vulcan Mind Melt in
Star Trek where you get a character in your

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:24.949
mind and you do some deep breathing and you
get yourself so you are transitioning out

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:29.199
of the busy world and you’re going to be
here now with this character and you set a

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:34.689
timer for like 15 minutes, you put your fingers
on the keyboard or pen on paper and you close

00:04:34.689 --> 00:04:37.979
your eyes and you just type.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:42.490
And your mind will go “I’ve got to get
groceries or the cat or whatever.”

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.499
Just let it go, you don’t get attached to
it and just keep coming back this character

00:04:46.499 --> 00:04:48.800
and you get done with this.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.509
And so you have all of these words that you’ve
typed out.

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:56.800
Some of them are like dialogue (like a monologue)
and some of them are like a stream of consciousness.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.400
80 percent of it may seem like gobbledygook
to you (maybe 90 percent) but 20 percent or

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.430
10 percent there is something really interesting
that popped out there and that is getting

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:15.409
in touch with the characters at a subconscious
level and they start to talk to you.

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:17.389
I’ve seen this time and time again.

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:23.669
I created a prep class 10 years ago that I
teach online, 6 week workshop taking the story

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:29.389
from concept to outline and the first four
weeks are all character development.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:30.759
The second week is brainstorming.

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:31.759
That’s all we do it brainstorming.

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:33.970
That’s all they do is brainstorm.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:39.569
And I’ve had hundreds of people tell me
that was the post eye opening experience for

00:05:39.569 --> 00:05:41.619
them was doing that type of work.

00:05:41.619 --> 00:05:47.039
Again the characters exist if you act on that
belief and we reach out to them and get them

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:52.139
talking to us.

00:05:52.139 --> 00:05:53.599
Those are great examples of character development.

00:05:53.599 --> 00:06:00.409
What can happen out of that is if you identify
a protagonist and their state of disunity

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:06.479
where they are disconnected from their authentic
nature and that can inform (if you know what

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:10.599
the disunity state is) that can inform where
the unity state is.

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:14.099
And so now they are not going to just jump
there.

00:06:14.099 --> 00:06:19.189
Then you are going to say “Okay so these
plot elements and the characters with whom

00:06:19.189 --> 00:06:25.449
they intersect they are going to deconstruct
their whole ways of being because they don’t

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:29.360
work out here in the new world but in the
process of that they are going to uncover

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.529
their need (their needs are going to start
to emerge).

00:06:31.529 --> 00:06:36.589
This is very Jungian, Jung talks about this,
he says “When an inner situation is not

00:06:36.589 --> 00:06:42.289
made conscious, (and he’s talking about
conflict, inner conflict) it happens outside

00:06:42.289 --> 00:06:43.289
as fate.”

00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:49.580
I don’t know if it’s in the real world
but that is the protagonist’s story.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:56.309
The protagonist starts off in a state of disunity
and what happens is informed by that, there

00:06:56.309 --> 00:06:59.129
is a synergy between them, it’s not just
these random events.

00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:03.399
It’s like these characters that they intersect
with and the events that they go through,

00:07:03.399 --> 00:07:06.500
that’s going to provide the basis of their
transformation.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.150
And so their disunity gets deconstructed.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:13.950
Now this stuff starts to come out in the light
of consciousness (their need) and now they

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.409
move into reconstruction.

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:20.900
Now they are moving into their new mode of
being and eventually toward unity.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:26.729
So you can take that raw material that you
get with your characters and you can see how

00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:31.669
it starts to shape the nature of the plot,
the story structure and it’s not just plugging

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:36.069
this in (this has to happen on page 25 the
break into Act 2).

00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:40.809
No…you are doing it in a much more organic
fashion with your characters letting them

00:07:40.809 --> 00:07:43.550
inform you what the structure will be.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:49.719
The final value of that is you’re writing
multilayer richly textured characters who

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:51.709
actors want to play.

00:07:51.709 --> 00:07:55.939
So character development to me is like everything
and it’s absolutely critical.

00:07:55.939 --> 00:08:01.749
Matthew Kalil, Screenwriter/Author/Instructor:
For me plot and the analysis of plot can be

00:08:01.749 --> 00:08:07.979
a real source of people getting stuck in their
writing because they feel they need to have

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:10.119
a plot that works before they write.

00:08:10.119 --> 00:08:14.629
Now if you just write without a plot you can
also end up down a dark hole of loose ends

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:17.680
that lead nowhere and just write the rest
of your life without having any structure

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:26.169
but I find plot and the analysis of plot a
good place to be once you’ve written something

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:34.050
but for me plot arises out of characters in
a space doing something and that creates the

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:35.050
plot.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:38.770
The characters themselves are almost…it’s
like this whole separation of character and

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:44.099
plot is not actually real when you are creating
it or watching it.

00:08:44.099 --> 00:08:50.040
Character and plot are intrinsically linked,
we separate them in order to analyze and maybe

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:55.410
in the re-write write our stories better but
I do find that if you focus on plot and drop

00:08:55.410 --> 00:09:04.400
characters it can result in stories that are…that
feel a bit empty and it can result in stories

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:05.580
that everyone has written before.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.470
What I find really funny about these books
on plot and some very good books are written

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:10.740
on plot and I point to them in my book.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.970
I don’t talk about plot at all in my book
[The Three Wells of Screenwriting].

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:18.620
What I find really funny about books on plot
is they will say “These seven beats need

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.090
to be in a story.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.420
They don’t have to be there in that order.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.260
They don’t all have to be there.”

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.450
And I’m like what do you mean they don’t
have to be there?

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:31.030
I don’t know if you’ve read closely but
there is almost a disclaimer that they don’t

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.390
have to be in that order and they don’t
have to necessarily all be there.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.320
And I’m like “Well what are you really
trying to say?”

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:40.510
So I’m slightly suspicious of generating
your idea writing from plot first.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:44.360
I think it can be really useful in the rewrite,
it’s definitely really useful in the story

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.360
edit.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.130
I use it all the time when I’m trying to
help writers strengthen their stories.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:55.660
But actually the best little plot that I’ve
ever heard, the best quote on plot is not

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:00.380
even about plot it’s from that book on filmmaking
that I mentioned earlier by Alexander Mackendrick

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:01.380
[On Filmmaking: An Introduction to the Craft
of the Director].

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:07.530
He says “The only thing you need to know,
what is happening now in story, should not

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:16.220
be as exciting or interesting as what may
or may not happen later.”

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:20.510
What is happening now should not be as exciting
or interesting as what may or may not happen

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:21.510
later.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:24.870
So something is happening later and it’s
drawing the story forward and it’s that

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.010
thing that is happening later that you need
to put in your plot somewhere.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:30.770
So you need to know that something is going
to happen, you need to set it up so that something

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:34.140
is going to pay off and it will drive the
story forward and that will keep people watching.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.940
I like that because it’s kind of an escapist…it’s
not really about plot but it’s kind of linked

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:40.940
to structure in some way.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:49.260
So yes I am not anti-plot, as a matter of
fact I am pro-plot when it comes to analysis

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:53.260
of the story and trying to make it stronger
because I do believe you have to take a sledgehammer

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:57.160
to your story once you’ve written it and
hit it and if it breaks something is wrong

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:59.380
with the structure and you need to make it
work.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:00.510
Professor Richard Walter, Author and UCLA
Screenwriting Program Chair: Character is

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.150
story as I’ve said before.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:06.010
What is a character other than what she does
and what she says.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:12.680
I have a friend who is a TV writer and he
has a writing partner (they call him) and

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:13.680
they collaborate.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.050
And somebody said “Oh…so you guys work
together.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:20.830
So you do the character and he does the story?”

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:22.770
As if you could do that separately?

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:29.490
Characters don’t exist outside of stories,
they exist only inside of stories.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:34.370
Again they are like Hamlet, they are what
they do and what they say so you can’t…when

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:39.520
people talk about a character driven movie,
they just mean a good movie.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:46.730
Let me just point out to you how many movies
and plays and great classics is just the name

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.610
of the protagonist of the main character.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:58.690
For example Hamlet, King Lear, Macbeth, Romeo
and Juliet, Julius Caesar.

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:02.960
How about we go back to the Greeks: Medea,
Oedipus.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:10.280
Look at the great movies, THE GODFATHER, CITIZEN
KANE.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:16.400
It’s not always that way but more often
than not it’s the name of the character

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:19.730
that is often the name of the movie (the name
of the protagonist) and I only mention it

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:24.970
in this context because I am trying to indicate
to you you can’t separate story and character.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:25.970
Which is more important?

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:31.560
That’s like asking somebody “What is more
important to you, your heart or your lungs?”

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:36.650
Sometimes in the law they refer to separately
to plot and something they call sequence of

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:37.700
events.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:41.340
But to me plot is the sequence of events.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:49.730
Plot, sequence of events, story - these are
synonyms at least in lay terms.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:53.600
And it’s the most important thing and the
heart is the most difficult and elusive thing

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:55.620
to achieve is a good story.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:02.860
I could paint for you a world of a wacky-crazy,
kind of a character and there are workshops

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:05.590
that actually do this.

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:11.360
You just invent characters about what kind
of tree she would be if she were a tree and

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.170
what kind of candy bar she would eat if she
ate a candy bar.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:20.590
And people feel all kind of…as they create
these things but it’s nonsense.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:26.730
I think it’s actually anti-useful because
it suggests that characters exists outside

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:35.910
of stories what I want to hear is when students
pitch their stories in my class they are orally

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:43.500
telling the story to me and the eight writers
around the table including themselves.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.380
What I want to hear is what do we see and
what do we hear.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:52.590
I don’t want to hear that “Harry enters,
he had a job years ago and he never got along

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:53.870
with his mother.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:56.140
He’s the kind of guy that…”

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:57.140
Please!

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.670
Just tell me…I mean how is the audience
sitting in the movie theater going to get

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:02.760
that.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.220
They are going to get that from what they
see and what they hear.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.380
Nobody is going to be sitting next to them
whispering in their ear “Let me tell you

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:10.590
about this part and what I was trying to do
here.”

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:16.110
So that is what I want to see in the class
from the writer “We see this, we hear that.”

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:23.740
That’s what readers for agents and production
companies want to see on the page “We see

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:24.740
this, we hear that.”

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.970
That’s all there is and everything else
has to come out of that.

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:30.370
Peter Desberg, Author [NOW THAT’S FUNNY],
Screenwriter and Psychologist: So finally

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:31.630
in answer to your question.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:35.510
Jeffrey Davis, Author [NOW THAT’S FUNNY],
Screenwriter, Loyola Marymount Professor:

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:38.220
Yes, I knew we’d get there eventually.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.770
Film Courage: I’m patient.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:45.990
Peter Desberg: If you gave us a premise we
would eagerly take a shot at it knowing that

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.529
where we started would not be the place where
it ended.

00:14:49.529 --> 00:14:52.210
And it was particularly fascinating in the
book [NOW THAT’S FUNNY!

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:56.000
The Art and Craft of Writing Comedy] where
we had teams working where they would just

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.290
start riffing and the other would pick it
up and they would go back and then the other

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:01.490
would go back and they would just go back
and forth.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:06.360
Jeffrey Davis: Or they would change it, they
would say we don’t know anything about mothers

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:12.170
and daughters but fathers and sons we know
a lot about so let’s make it a father and

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:13.170
son.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:19.690
In one case (my favorite interview) the grandparents
are just lightly mentioned in the premise

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:25.779
and Lou Schneider made it a story more about
the grandparents and the mother than the daughter.

00:15:25.779 --> 00:15:29.150
The daughter kind of got lost in it.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:36.360
So that’s the other thing is I think that
the book does something that I’ve never

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:37.380
seen a book do before.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:43.440
It replicates (not because of us, because
of the writers), it replicates what a writers

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:48.910
room would be like because a writer’s room
you come in, you might have the arc of the

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:55.800
season, you might have those things, but you
need episodes along that line and they’ll

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.040
pitch an idea and somebody will go “That’s
not going to work.”

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:02.590
Or “We did that three years ago,” or blah,
blah, blah whatever it is.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:08.339
But ideas come in the room and as Peter said
they are not the same at the end as when they

00:16:08.339 --> 00:16:09.339
started.

00:16:09.339 --> 00:16:13.380
Peter Desberg: Now I’m going to take us
back to your question.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:20.880
When Jeffrey and I were starting we took a
slightly academic view one day and said “What’s

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.580
your prediction about the approach they’re
going to take?

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:28.050
Are they going to go more for story or are
they going to go more for character?”

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:34.710
And we found out the answer is neither, they
went for conflict.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:40.339
So if you were to give us a premise to develop,
the very first step we would take is “Okay

00:16:40.339 --> 00:16:43.440
whatever the premise is, where do we get conflict
out of it?”

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.350
If there are two characters involved, how
can we make them but heads to do something

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.529
because everything would come out of that.

00:16:50.529 --> 00:16:53.430
Scott Myers: Again, there is not one right
way to do this.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:17:00.170
I’m just saying that this I think is a smart
way to go about it because the characters,

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:01.170
it’s their story.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.199
Jill Chamberlain: You know I sometimes say
that there are two kinds of writers.

00:17:04.199 --> 00:17:07.049
The ones that start with character and the
ones that start with plot.

00:17:07.049 --> 00:17:08.990
I think that both are valid ways.

